@node ASSIGNMENT Statement
@section ASSIGNMENT Statement (Pascal :=)

@cindex assignment operator
@cindex :=
@cindex MOVE TO

@subheading Purpose

The ASSIGNMENT statement stores the result of an expression into a variable,
array element, or record field. Assignment is the fundamental mechanism for
state change in Pascal programs.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@var{target} := @var{expression}
MOVE @var{expression} TO @var{target}
@end example

The @code{:=} operator is the primary assignment operator. The @code{MOVE TO}
syntax provides an alternative, COBOL-like syntax for compatibility.

@subheading Numeric Type Considerations

Assignment handles all numeric types: BINARY (signed/unsigned), DECIMAL (BCD
signed/unsigned), and DISPLAY (character-encoded digits).

@subsubheading BINARY Types

BINARY numbers are machine-native integers, typically 16-bit or 32-bit:

@example
Score := 100              @r{BINARY unsigned}
Temperature := -40        @r{BINARY signed}
Counter := Counter + 1    @r{BINARY arithmetic}
@end example

@subsubheading DECIMAL Types

DECIMAL numbers use Binary Coded Decimal (BCD) encoding, where each digit
occupies 4 bits. Signed DECIMAL uses a sign nibble:

@example
Price := 9999            @r{DECIMAL unsigned (DISPLAY)}
Amount := 12345          @r{DECIMAL signed}
@end example

@subsubheading DISPLAY Types

DISPLAY (also called ZONED) numbers store digits as character values (0x30-0x39
for digits 0-9):

@example
CardNumber := 4532        @r{DISPLAY (string-like)}
PIN := 1234              @r{DISPLAY encoded}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple BINARY Assignment}
PROCEDURE TestAssignment
BEGIN
  VAR score : INTEGER
  VAR lives : INTEGER
  score := 0
  lives := 3
  score := score + 100
END

@r{Example 2: DECIMAL Assignment with Precision}
PROCEDURE CalcTotal
BEGIN
  VAR price : DECIMAL
  VAR quantity : DECIMAL
  VAR total : DECIMAL
  price := 999
  quantity := 100
  total := price * quantity
  @r{result: total = 99900}
END

@r{Example 3: Record Field Assignment}
PROCEDURE UpdatePlayer
BEGIN
  VAR player_x : INTEGER
  VAR player_y : INTEGER
  player_x := 160
  player_y := 100
  player_x := player_x + 5
END

@r{Example 4: Array Element Assignment}
PROCEDURE InitScores
BEGIN
  VAR scores : ARRAY
  VAR i : INTEGER
  FOR i FROM 0 TO 9 STEP 1 NEXT
    scores[i] := 0
  END
END

@r{Example 5: Type Conversion Assignment}
PROCEDURE MixTypes
BEGIN
  VAR binary_val : INTEGER
  VAR decimal_val : DECIMAL
  binary_val := 255
  decimal_val := binary_val    @r{conversion}
  binary_val := decimal_val    @r{conversion back}
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Overflow:} Assigning a value larger than the target type can hold
may wrap or truncate. BINARY values wrap; DECIMAL may overflow the sign nibble.

@item @strong{Type Mismatch:} Implicit conversion occurs between numeric types.
BINARY to DECIMAL may lose precision; DECIMAL to BINARY truncates.

@item @strong{Null Assignment:} Assigning nil/undefined values results in
zero or undefined behavior depending on the backend.

@item @strong{Chain Assignment:} Not directly supported; use multiple statements.

@item @strong{Subscript Bounds:} Array assignment does not check bounds;
out-of-range indices may corrupt memory.

@item @strong{Undefined Variables:} Assigning to undeclared variables may
silently succeed or fail depending on the backend.
@end itemize

@node IF/THEN/ELSE Statement
@section IF/THEN/ELSE Statement

@cindex IF THEN ELSE
@cindex conditional execution
@cindex ELSE IF

@subheading Purpose

The IF/THEN/ELSE statement conditionally executes one of two statement blocks
based on the truth value of a condition expression.

@subheading Syntax

@example
IF @var{condition} THEN
  @var{then-statements}
ELSE
  @var{else-statements}
END

IF @var{condition} THEN
  @var{then-statements}
ELSE IF @var{condition2} THEN
  @var{then-statements2}
ELSE
  @var{else-statements}
END
@end example

The @code{ELSE} clause is optional. Nested IF statements are supported.

@subheading Condition Evaluation

Conditions are boolean expressions using relational (<, >, =, <>), logical (AND, OR, NOT),
and equality operators. All numeric types support comparisons.

@subheading Numeric Type Considerations

Comparisons work across all numeric types with implicit conversion:

@example
IF score > 100 THEN           @r{BINARY comparison}
IF price = 999 THEN           @r{DECIMAL comparison}
IF binary_val = decimal_val THEN  @r{cross-type}
@end example

Unsigned comparisons are straightforward. Signed comparisons use the sign bit/nibble.
Mixed-type comparisons convert to a common representation.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple IF}
PROCEDURE CheckScore
BEGIN
  VAR score : INTEGER
  score := 150
  IF score > 100 THEN
    PRINT "High Score!"
  END
END

@r{Example 2: IF with ELSE}
PROCEDURE CheckHealth
BEGIN
  VAR health : INTEGER
  health := 30
  IF health > 0 THEN
    PRINT "Still alive"
  ELSE
    PRINT "Game Over"
  END
END

@r{Example 3: Nested IF}
PROCEDURE GradeStudent
BEGIN
  VAR score : INTEGER
  score := 85
  IF score >= 90 THEN
    PRINT "A"
  ELSE IF score >= 80 THEN
    PRINT "B"
  ELSE IF score >= 70 THEN
    PRINT "C"
  ELSE
    PRINT "F"
  END
END

@r{Example 4: Complex Condition}
PROCEDURE CheckGameState
BEGIN
  VAR lives : INTEGER
  VAR score : INTEGER
  VAR level : INTEGER
  lives := 3
  score := 500
  level := 2
  IF lives > 0 AND score > 100 THEN
    IF level <= 5 THEN
      PRINT "Continue"
    END
  END
END

@r{Example 5: DECIMAL Comparison}
PROCEDURE PriceCheck
BEGIN
  VAR price : DECIMAL
  VAR budget : DECIMAL
  price := 5000
  budget := 10000
  IF price <= budget THEN
    PRINT "Affordable"
  ELSE
    PRINT "Too expensive"
  END
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Empty Branches:} Both THEN and ELSE branches require at least one
statement (use GOBACK or proceed to next if no action needed).

@item @strong{Floating-Point Conditions:} Equality tests (= and <>) on floating
point values may fail due to precision; use relational operators instead.

@item @strong{Unsigned Overflow:} Comparing unsigned values near their limits
works correctly; negative checks fail for unsigned types.

@item @strong{String Comparisons:} String comparisons use lexicographic ordering;
numeric strings compare as strings, not numbers.

@item @strong{Logical Short-Circuit:} AND and OR do not guarantee short-circuit
evaluation; all operands may evaluate.
@end itemize

@node WHILE Loop
@section WHILE Loop (Pre-Test)

@cindex WHILE loop
@cindex pre-test loop
@cindex loop condition

@subheading Purpose

The WHILE loop repeatedly executes a block of statements while a condition remains
true. The condition is tested before each iteration (pre-test loop).

@subheading Syntax

@example
WHILE @var{condition} NEXT
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

The @code{NEXT} keyword marks the body. If the condition is false initially,
the body never executes.

@subheading Numeric Types in Loop Conditions

Loop conditions support all numeric types:

@example
WHILE counter > 0 NEXT        @r{BINARY counter}
WHILE distance < 1000 NEXT    @r{BINARY distance}
WHILE amount > 0 NEXT         @r{DECIMAL amount}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Counter Loop}
PROCEDURE CountDown
BEGIN
  VAR counter : INTEGER
  counter := 10
  WHILE counter > 0 NEXT
    PRINT counter
    counter := counter - 1
  END
  PRINT "Blastoff!"
END

@r{Example 2: DECIMAL Loop with Decrement}
PROCEDURE LoopDecimal
BEGIN
  VAR amount : DECIMAL
  amount := 5000
  WHILE amount > 1000 NEXT
    amount := amount - 500
    PRINT amount
  END
END

@r{Example 3: Early Exit with EXIT}
PROCEDURE SearchLoop
BEGIN
  VAR index : INTEGER
  VAR found : BOOLEAN
  index := 0
  found := 0
  WHILE index < 100 NEXT
    IF items[index] = target THEN
      found := 1
      EXIT
    END
    index := index + 1
  END
END

@r{Example 4: Nested WHILE Loops}
PROCEDURE MatrixPrint
BEGIN
  VAR row : INTEGER
  VAR col : INTEGER
  row := 0
  WHILE row < 3 NEXT
    col := 0
    WHILE col < 3 NEXT
      PRINT matrix[row][col]
      col := col + 1
    END
    row := row + 1
  END
END

@r{Example 5: Complex Condition}
PROCEDURE MultiCondition
BEGIN
  VAR x : INTEGER
  VAR y : INTEGER
  VAR running : BOOLEAN
  x := 0
  y := 100
  running := 1
  WHILE x < y AND running > 0 NEXT
    PRINT x
    x := x + 10
    IF x = 50 THEN
      running := 0
    END
  END
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Infinite Loop:} A condition that never becomes false causes an
infinite loop; use EXIT or modify the condition inside the loop.

@item @strong{Never-Executing Body:} If the condition is false before the first
iteration, the body never executes.

@item @strong{Unsigned Underflow:} Decrementing an unsigned counter near zero
may wrap to the maximum value.

@item @strong{Floating-Point Precision:} Loop conditions with floating-point
arithmetic may exit unexpectedly due to rounding errors.

@item @strong{Modified Loop Variable:} Modifying the loop condition variable
inside the loop is allowed but may cause unexpected behavior.
@end itemize

@node REPEAT/UNTIL Loop
@section REPEAT/UNTIL Loop (Post-Test)

@cindex REPEAT loop
@cindex post-test loop
@cindex UNTIL condition

@subheading Purpose

The REPEAT/UNTIL loop executes a block of statements, then tests a condition.
The body always executes at least once (post-test loop). The loop exits when
the condition becomes true.

@subheading Syntax

@example
REPEAT
  @var{statements}
UNTIL @var{condition}
@end example

Note the inversion: REPEAT/UNTIL continues while the condition is @strong{false}
and exits when it becomes @strong{true}.

@subheading Numeric Type Considerations

UNTIL conditions support all numeric types:

@example
REPEAT
  counter := counter - 1
UNTIL counter = 0           @r{BINARY}

REPEAT
  amount := amount - 100
UNTIL amount < 1000         @r{DECIMAL}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Password Validation}
PROCEDURE PasswordPrompt
BEGIN
  VAR attempts : INTEGER
  VAR password : STRING
  VAR correct : BOOLEAN
  attempts := 0
  REPEAT
    INPUT password
    IF password = "secret" THEN
      correct := 1
    ELSE
      correct := 0
    END
    attempts := attempts + 1
  UNTIL correct = 1 OR attempts >= 3
END

@r{Example 2: Numeric Processing}
PROCEDURE ProcessAmount
BEGIN
  VAR remaining : DECIMAL
  VAR total : DECIMAL
  remaining := 10000
  total := 0
  REPEAT
    total := total + 100
    remaining := remaining - 100
  UNTIL remaining = 0
  PRINT "Total: " + total
END

@r{Example 3: Game Loop (at least one frame)}
PROCEDURE GameFrame
BEGIN
  VAR frame_count : INTEGER
  VAR running : BOOLEAN
  frame_count := 0
  running := 1
  REPEAT
    ProcessInput
    UpdateGame
    RenderDisplay
    frame_count := frame_count + 1
    IF quit_requested = 1 THEN
      running := 0
    END
  UNTIL running = 0
END

@r{Example 4: Boundary Search}
PROCEDURE FindBoundary
BEGIN
  VAR value : INTEGER
  VAR threshold : INTEGER
  threshold := 500
  value := 1
  REPEAT
    value := value * 2
  UNTIL value > threshold
  PRINT "Boundary: " + value
END

@r{Example 5: String Processing}
PROCEDURE ProcessString
BEGIN
  VAR char : CHAR
  VAR index : INTEGER
  index := 0
  REPEAT
    char := str[index]
    ProcessChar char
    index := index + 1
  UNTIL index >= length
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Guaranteed Execution:} Unlike WHILE, the body always executes at least once,
even if the condition is initially true.

@item @strong{Condition Inversion:} UNTIL exits when true (opposite of WHILE which continues
while true). Be careful not to confuse the logic.

@item @strong{Unsigned Boundary:} REPEAT with unsigned counters that underflow wrap to
the maximum value, potentially creating infinite loops.

@item @strong{Floating-Point Precision:} Floating-point loop conditions may exhibit
unexpected behavior due to rounding.

@item @strong{Memory Modification:} If memory is modified inside the loop in ways
that affect the condition, the loop behavior changes unpredictably.
@end itemize

@node FOR Loop
@section FOR Loop (Counting Loop)

@cindex FOR loop
@cindex counting loop
@cindex loop variable
@cindex loop step

@subheading Purpose

The FOR loop iterates a fixed number of times using an explicit loop variable
that takes on successive values in a sequence from start to end with a given step.

@subheading Syntax

@example
FOR @var{variable} FROM @var{start} TO @var{end} STEP @var{step} NEXT
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

The @code{STEP} clause is optional; the default step is 1. Negative steps are supported
for counting downward (use @code{DOWNTO} or negative @code{STEP}).

@subheading Numeric Type Considerations

FOR loops support all numeric types for start, end, and step:

@example
FOR i FROM 1 TO 10 STEP 1 NEXT      @r{BINARY}
FOR x FROM 0 TO 9999 STEP 100 NEXT  @r{DECIMAL}
FOR j FROM 100 DOWNTO 0 STEP -10 NEXT @r{negative step}
@end example

Type conversions occur automatically when mixing types.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Forward Loop}
PROCEDURE CountTo10
BEGIN
  VAR i : INTEGER
  FOR i FROM 1 TO 10 STEP 1 NEXT
    PRINT i
  END
END

@r{Example 2: Counting Down}
PROCEDURE Countdown
BEGIN
  VAR i : INTEGER
  FOR i FROM 10 DOWNTO 1 STEP -1 NEXT
    PRINT i
  END
  PRINT "Blast off!"
END

@r{Example 3: Initialize Array}
PROCEDURE ClearArray
BEGIN
  VAR arr : ARRAY
  VAR i : INTEGER
  FOR i FROM 0 TO 99 STEP 1 NEXT
    arr[i] := 0
  END
END

@r{Example 4: Large Step}
PROCEDURE LoopLargeStep
BEGIN
  VAR value : INTEGER
  FOR value FROM 0 TO 1000 STEP 100 NEXT
    PRINT value
  END
END

@r{Example 5: DECIMAL Range}
PROCEDURE DecimalRange
BEGIN
  VAR amount : DECIMAL
  FOR amount FROM 100 TO 1000 STEP 100 NEXT
    PRINT amount
  END
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Never-Executing Loop:} If start > end with positive step,
the loop body never executes.

@item @strong{Infinite Loop:} If step is zero or has the wrong sign, the loop
may never terminate.

@item @strong{Overflow:} Loop variables near the maximum value may overflow
when incremented.

@item @strong{Floating-Point Loops:} FOR loops with floating-point values may
exhibit precision errors due to rounding.

@item @strong{Modified Loop Variable:} Modifying the loop variable inside the
loop body causes unpredictable behavior; the next iteration's value depends
on how the variable was modified.
@end itemize

@node CASE Statement
@section CASE Statement (Multi-Way Branch)

@cindex CASE statement
@cindex switch statement
@cindex multi-way branch
@cindex ENDCASE

@subheading Purpose

The CASE statement selects one of multiple statement blocks based on the value
of an expression. It replaces multiple IF/ELSE IF chains for cleaner code.

@subheading Syntax

@example
CASE @var{expression} OF
  @var{value1} : @var{statements}
  @var{value2} : @var{statements}
  @var{value3}, @var{value4} : @var{statements}
  ELSE : @var{default-statements}
ENDCASE
@end example

Each case value must be a constant expression. Multiple values can share
the same statements. The @code{ELSE} clause is optional.

@subheading Numeric Type Considerations

Case expressions support all numeric types:

@example
CASE direction OF                    @r{BINARY expression}
  1 : MoveNorth
  2 : MoveSouth
  3 : MoveEast
  4 : MoveWest
ENDCASE

CASE price OF                        @r{DECIMAL expression}
  100 : ProcessExpensive
  10, 20, 30 : ProcessStandard
  ELSE : ProcessCheap
ENDCASE
@end example

Type conversion occurs when comparing case values with the expression.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Direction Handling}
PROCEDURE HandleDirection
BEGIN
  VAR dir : INTEGER
  dir := 2
  CASE dir OF
    1 : MoveNorth
    2 : MoveSouth
    3 : MoveEast
    4 : MoveWest
    ELSE : Beep
  ENDCASE
END

@r{Example 2: Price Tiers}
PROCEDURE CalculateDiscount
BEGIN
  VAR price : DECIMAL
  price := 5000
  CASE price OF
    9999 : discount := 30
    5000 : discount := 20
    1000, 1500, 2000 : discount := 10
    ELSE : discount := 0
  ENDCASE
END

@r{Example 3: Game State}
PROCEDURE GameState
BEGIN
  VAR state : INTEGER
  state := 1
  CASE state OF
    0 : MainMenu
    1 : GameRunning
    2 : PauseMenu
    3 : GameOver
  ENDCASE
END

@r{Example 4: Multiple Values}
PROCEDURE ProcessVowel
BEGIN
  VAR char : CHAR
  char := "a"
  CASE char OF
    "a", "e", "i", "o", "u" : ProcessVowel
    ELSE : ProcessConsonant
  ENDCASE
END

@r{Example 5: Large Range}
PROCEDURE RangeHandler
BEGIN
  VAR value : INTEGER
  value := 5
  CASE value OF
    1, 2, 3, 4, 5 : ProcessSmall
    6, 7, 8, 9, 10 : ProcessMedium
    ELSE : ProcessLarge
  ENDCASE
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{No Matching Case:} If no case matches and there's no @code{ELSE},
no statements execute.

@item @strong{Fall-Through:} Unlike C, Pascal cases do @strong{not} fall through;
each case is independent.

@item @strong{Case Range:} Case values must be discrete constants; ranges like
@code{1..5} are not supported (use multiple values or nested IFs).

@item @strong{String Cases:} Case expressions on strings use character-by-character
comparison; ensure string values match exactly (including case).

@item @strong{Duplicate Cases:} Duplicate case values cause undefined behavior;
the first matching case executes.
@end itemize

@node PROCEDURE Declaration
@section PROCEDURE Declaration

@cindex PROCEDURE
@cindex subroutine
@cindex method
@cindex ON keyword

@subheading Purpose

A PROCEDURE declaration defines a named block of code that can be called multiple
times from different locations. Procedures encapsulate logic and enable code reuse.

@subheading Syntax

@example
PROCEDURE @var{name}
BEGIN
  @var{statements}
END

PROCEDURE @var{name} ON @var{object}
BEGIN
  @var{statements}
END

PROCEDURE @var{name} IN LIBRARY
BEGIN
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

The @code{ON} keyword associates the procedure with an object or class.
The @code{IN LIBRARY} keyword marks the procedure as available for inter-bank calls.

@subheading Variable Scope

Variables declared within a procedure are local to that procedure.
Global variables (declared outside all procedures) are accessible within procedures.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Procedure}
PROCEDURE InitGame
BEGIN
  Score := 0
  Lives := 3
  Level := 1
END

@r{Example 2: Procedure with Object Association}
PROCEDURE Move ON Player
BEGIN
  X := X + DeltaX
  Y := Y + DeltaY
END

@r{Example 3: Library Procedure}
PROCEDURE DrawSprite IN LIBRARY
BEGIN
  SetSpriteData
  RenderToScreen
END

@r{Example 4: Procedure Calling Another}
PROCEDURE GameLoop
BEGIN
  ProcessInput
  UpdateGameState
  RenderFrame
END

PROCEDURE ProcessInput
BEGIN
  @r{handle keyboard, joystick, etc}
END

@r{Example 5: Multiple Procedures}
PROCEDURE Main
BEGIN
  InitGame
  WHILE running NEXT
    GameLoop
  END
  CleanupGame
END

PROCEDURE InitGame
BEGIN
  @r{initialization}
END

PROCEDURE GameLoop
BEGIN
  @r{main loop}
END

PROCEDURE CleanupGame
BEGIN
  @r{cleanup}
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Recursive Calls:} Procedures can call themselves recursively,
but stack overflow is possible with deep recursion.

@item @strong{Undefined Procedure:} Calling a procedure before its declaration
may fail depending on the frontend; use forward declarations if needed.

@item @strong{Name Collision:} Procedures with the same name as built-in keywords
may cause parsing errors.

@item @strong{Empty Procedure:} A procedure with no statements is allowed but
serves no purpose.

@item @strong{Global State Modification:} Procedures can modify global variables,
making the program's behavior harder to reason about.
@end itemize

@node FUNCTION Declaration
@section FUNCTION Declaration

@cindex FUNCTION
@cindex function result
@cindex RETURN value

@subheading Purpose

A FUNCTION declaration defines a named block of code that returns a value.
Functions are similar to procedures but explicitly produce a result.

@subheading Syntax

@example
FUNCTION @var{name} : @var{return-type}
BEGIN
  @var{statements}
  RETURN @var{expression}
END
@end example

The return type is specified after the colon. The function exits and returns
a value via the @code{RETURN} statement.

@subheading Numeric Type Return Values

Functions can return all numeric types:

@example
FUNCTION GetScore : INTEGER
BEGIN
  RETURN Score
END

FUNCTION CalculateTotal : DECIMAL
BEGIN
  RETURN Price * Quantity
END
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Integer Function}
FUNCTION GetScore : INTEGER
BEGIN
  RETURN Score
END

@r{Example 2: DECIMAL Calculation}
FUNCTION CalculateDiscount : DECIMAL
BEGIN
  RETURN BasePrice * DiscountPercent / 100
END

@r{Example 3: Boolean Return}
FUNCTION IsGameOver : BOOLEAN
BEGIN
  IF Lives <= 0 OR Level > MaxLevel THEN
    RETURN 1
  ELSE
    RETURN 0
  END
END

@r{Example 4: Conditional Return}
FUNCTION MaxValue : INTEGER
BEGIN
  IF ValueA > ValueB THEN
    RETURN ValueA
  ELSE
    RETURN ValueB
  END
END

@r{Example 5: Complex Calculation}
FUNCTION Distance : INTEGER
BEGIN
  VAR dx : INTEGER
  VAR dy : INTEGER
  dx := PlayerX - TargetX
  dy := PlayerY - TargetY
  RETURN dx + dy
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{No Return:} If a function reaches END without executing RETURN,
the return value is undefined.

@item @strong{Multiple Returns:} A function can have multiple RETURN statements
at different paths; the first executed return is used.

@item @strong{Type Mismatch:} Returning a value incompatible with the declared
return type causes conversion or error.

@item @strong{Early Return:} RETURN can appear anywhere in the function body,
allowing early exit.

@item @strong{Recursive Functions:} Functions can call themselves recursively,
with risk of stack overflow.
@end itemize

@node PROCEDURE Calls
@section PROCEDURE Calls

@cindex CALL statement
@cindex procedure invocation
@cindex method invocation
@cindex ON keyword

@subheading Purpose

PROCEDURE CALL statements invoke previously declared procedures or external routines.

@subheading Syntax

@example
CALL @var{procedure-name}
CALL @var{procedure-name} ON @var{object}
CALL @var{procedure-name} IN @var{bank}
CALL @var{procedure-name} IN LIBRARY
@end example

The @code{CALL} keyword is optional; a bare procedure name is also a call.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Procedure Call}
PROCEDURE MainLoop
BEGIN
  ProcessInput
  UpdateGame
  RenderDisplay
END

@r{Example 2: Method Invocation}
PROCEDURE UpdateAll
BEGIN
  CALL Move ON Player
  CALL Draw ON Sprite1
  CALL Update ON GameObject
END

@r{Example 3: Bank Call}
PROCEDURE AccessBank
BEGIN
  CALL LoadResource IN 1
  CALL DecompressData IN 2
END

@r{Example 4: Library Call}
PROCEDURE DrawGraphics
BEGIN
  CALL DrawSprite IN LIBRARY
  CALL UpdateScreen IN LIBRARY
END

@r{Example 5: Conditional Call}
PROCEDURE MaybeUpdatePlayer
BEGIN
  IF PlayerActive = 1 THEN
    CALL Move ON Player
  END
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Undefined Procedure:} Calling a non-existent procedure results
in a compile error or runtime error.

@item @strong{Infinite Recursion:} A procedure calling itself directly or indirectly
with no base case causes stack overflow.

@item @strong{Side Effects:} Called procedures may modify global state, affecting
subsequent code behavior.

@item @strong{Bank Context:} Bank calls may fail if the target bank is not loaded
or is not in memory.
@end itemize

@node FUNCTION Calls
@section FUNCTION Calls

@cindex function call
@cindex function result
@cindex assignment from function

@subheading Purpose

FUNCTION CALL expressions invoke functions and use their return values in expressions or assignments.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@var{result} := @var{function-name}
IF @var{function-name} > 0 THEN
  @var{statements}
END
@end example

Functions can be called in any expression context.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Assignment from Function}
PROCEDURE GetValues
BEGIN
  VAR score : INTEGER
  score := GetScore
  PRINT score
END

@r{Example 2: Function in Condition}
PROCEDURE CheckDistance
BEGIN
  IF Distance > 100 THEN
    ProcessFar
  ELSE
    ProcessNear
  END
END

@r{Example 3: Nested Function Calls}
PROCEDURE CalculateTotal
BEGIN
  VAR total : DECIMAL
  total := GetPrice + GetTax
  PRINT total
END

@r{Example 4: Function Result in Expression}
PROCEDURE ComputeBonus
BEGIN
  VAR bonus : INTEGER
  bonus := GetScore / 10
  PRINT bonus
END

@r{Example 5: Function in Loop}
PROCEDURE ProcessUntilDone
BEGIN
  WHILE ProcessNextItem > 0 NEXT
    ContinueProcessing
  END
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Side Effects:} Functions may modify global state as a side effect;
the result value is what matters, but state changes happen too.

@item @strong{Performance:} Function calls have overhead; frequent calls in loops
may be slow.

@item @strong{Order of Evaluation:} In expressions with multiple function calls,
the evaluation order is unspecified (be careful with interdependent functions).
@end itemize

@node BEGIN/END Blocks
@section BEGIN/END Blocks

@cindex BEGIN
@cindex END
@cindex block structure
@cindex statement grouping

@subheading Purpose

BEGIN/END blocks group multiple statements into a single unit. They are essential
for the body of procedures, functions, loops, and conditional statements.

@subheading Syntax

@example
BEGIN
  @var{statement1}
  @var{statement2}
  @var{statement3}
END
@end example

Statements within a block are separated by line breaks or semicolons.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Procedure Block}
PROCEDURE InitGame
BEGIN
  Score := 0
  Lives := 3
  Level := 1
END

@r{Example 2: Loop Block}
FOR i FROM 1 TO 10 STEP 1 NEXT
BEGIN
  Score := Score + 10
  PRINT Score
END

@r{Example 3: Conditional Block}
IF Health > 0 THEN
BEGIN
  ProcessFrame
  UpdateDisplay
END
END

@r{Example 4: Nested Blocks}
BEGIN
  BEGIN
    Score := Score + 100
  END
  PRINT Score
END

@r{Example 5: Block with Mixed Statements}
BEGIN
  VAR temp : INTEGER
  temp := 0
  WHILE temp < 10 NEXT
    temp := temp + 1
  END
  PRINT temp
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Empty Block:} A BEGIN/END with no statements is allowed.

@item @strong{Nested Blocks:} Blocks can be nested arbitrarily deep.

@item @strong{Variable Scope:} Variables declared in a block are visible throughout
the block and nested blocks.

@item @strong{Statement Separators:} Semicolons between statements are optional
in most cases; line breaks suffice.
@end itemize

@node VAR Declarations
@section VAR Declarations (Variable Declaration)

@cindex VAR
@cindex variable declaration
@cindex variable scope
@cindex type annotation

@subheading Purpose

VAR declarations define named variables that store values. Variables must be
declared before use (in most contexts).

@subheading Syntax

@example
VAR @var{name} : @var{type}
VAR @var{name1}, @var{name2} : @var{type}
@end example

Multiple variables of the same type can be declared on one line.

@subheading Basic Types

@table @asis
@item @code{INTEGER} - signed machine integer
@item @code{DECIMAL} - binary-coded decimal
@item @code{DISPLAY} - character-encoded decimal
@item @code{STRING} - character string
@item @code{BOOLEAN} - true/false value
@item @code{CHAR} - single character
@item @code{REAL} - floating-point number
@end table

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Variables}
PROCEDURE TestVars
BEGIN
  VAR score : INTEGER
  VAR name : STRING
  VAR active : BOOLEAN
  score := 100
  name := "Player"
  active := 1
END

@r{Example 2: Multiple Variables}
PROCEDURE TestMultiple
BEGIN
  VAR x, y, z : INTEGER
  x := 10
  y := 20
  z := x + y
END

@r{Example 3: DECIMAL Variables}
PROCEDURE TestDecimal
BEGIN
  VAR price, quantity, total : DECIMAL
  price := 1000
  quantity := 10
  total := price * quantity
END

@r{Example 4: Array Variable}
PROCEDURE TestArray
BEGIN
  VAR scores : ARRAY
  scores[0] := 100
  scores[1] := 200
END

@r{Example 5: Mixed Types}
PROCEDURE TestMixed
BEGIN
  VAR binary : INTEGER
  VAR decimal : DECIMAL
  VAR text : STRING
  binary := 42
  decimal := 9999
  text := "Result"
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Shadowing:} A variable in an inner scope with the same name as
an outer scope variable shadows (hides) the outer variable.

@item @strong{Uninitialized Variables:} Variables have undefined initial values;
explicit initialization is recommended.

@item @strong{Global Variables:} Variables declared outside procedures are global
and visible to all procedures.

@item @strong{Type Conversion:} Assigning a value of one type to a variable of
another type performs implicit conversion.
@end itemize

@node CONST Declarations
@section CONST Declarations (Constant Declaration)

@cindex CONST
@cindex constant
@cindex manifest constant
@cindex compile-time constant

@subheading Purpose

CONST declarations define named constants whose values cannot be changed after initialization.

@subheading Syntax

@example
CONST @var{name} = @var{expression}
CONST @var{name1} = @var{expr1}, @var{name2} = @var{expr2}
@end example

Constants are typically used for configuration values, magic numbers, and other
immutable values.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Constants}
PROCEDURE UseConst
BEGIN
  CONST MAX_SCORE = 10000
  CONST MIN_LIVES = 1
  IF Score > MAX_SCORE THEN
    PRINT "Record!"
  END
END

@r{Example 2: Multiple Constants}
PROCEDURE GameConfig
BEGIN
  CONST SCREEN_WIDTH = 320
  CONST SCREEN_HEIGHT = 200
  CONST MAX_SPRITES = 32
  CONST MAX_SOUNDS = 16
  InitDisplay SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT
END

@r{Example 3: DECIMAL Constants}
PROCEDURE PriceConst
BEGIN
  CONST BASE_PRICE = 9999
  CONST TAX_RATE = 100
  total := BASE_PRICE + (BASE_PRICE * TAX_RATE / 100)
END

@r{Example 4: String Constants}
PROCEDURE Messages
BEGIN
  CONST WELCOME = "Welcome to the Game"
  CONST GAME_OVER = "Game Over"
  CONST HIGH_SCORE = "New High Score!"
  PRINT WELCOME
END

@r{Example 5: Calculated Constants}
PROCEDURE Geometry
BEGIN
  CONST WIDTH = 160
  CONST HEIGHT = 100
  CONST AREA = WIDTH * HEIGHT
  CONST CENTER_X = WIDTH / 2
  CONST CENTER_Y = HEIGHT / 2
  InitGeometry AREA
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Reassignment:} Attempting to assign to a constant after initialization
results in a compile error or is silently ignored.

@item @strong{Scope:} Constants have the same scope rules as variables;
constants are typically declared at the procedure level or globally.

@item @strong{Type Inference:} The type of a constant is inferred from its initializer
expression.
@end itemize

@node TYPE Declarations
@section TYPE Declarations (Type Definition)

@cindex TYPE
@cindex type definition
@cindex type alias
@cindex record type

@subheading Purpose

TYPE declarations define new types based on existing types or create named aliases
for frequently-used type signatures.

@subheading Syntax

@example
TYPE @var{name} = @var{base-type}
TYPE @var{name} = RECORD
  @var{field1} : @var{type1}
  @var{field2} : @var{type2}
END
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Type Alias}
PROCEDURE TypeAlias
BEGIN
  TYPE Score = INTEGER
  VAR points : Score
  points := 100
END

@r{Example 2: Record Type}
PROCEDURE RecordType
BEGIN
  TYPE Player = RECORD
    x : INTEGER
    y : INTEGER
    health : INTEGER
  END
  VAR hero : Player
  hero.x := 160
  hero.y := 100
  hero.health := 100
END

@r{Example 3: Complex Record}
PROCEDURE ComplexRecord
BEGIN
  TYPE Sprite = RECORD
    x : INTEGER
    y : INTEGER
    frame : INTEGER
    active : BOOLEAN
  END
  VAR sprite1, sprite2 : Sprite
  sprite1.x := 0
  sprite1.y := 0
END

@r{Example 4: Nested Record}
PROCEDURE NestedRecord
BEGIN
  TYPE Position = RECORD
    x : INTEGER
    y : INTEGER
  END
  TYPE Entity = RECORD
    pos : Position
    health : INTEGER
  END
END

@r{Example 5: Array Type}
PROCEDURE ArrayType
BEGIN
  TYPE ScoreArray = ARRAY
  VAR scores : ScoreArray
  scores[0] := 100
  scores[1] := 200
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Forward References:} Records can reference other types defined
in the same scope.

@item @strong{Circular Types:} Circular type definitions (A contains B, B contains A)
are not allowed without pointer support.

@item @strong{Type Scope:} Type definitions have the same scope as variables;
types are visible to the rest of the scope after definition.
@end itemize

@node RECORD Type
@section RECORD Type (Structured Data)

@cindex RECORD type
@cindex field access
@cindex record variable
@cindex structure

@subheading Purpose

RECORD types group related data fields of different types into a single composite type.
Records provide structured access to related information.

@subheading Syntax

@example
TYPE @var{name} = RECORD
  @var{field1} : @var{type1}
  @var{field2} : @var{type2}
END

@var{record-var}.@var{field1} := @var{value}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Record}
PROCEDURE SimpleRecord
BEGIN
  TYPE Point = RECORD
    x : INTEGER
    y : INTEGER
  END
  VAR origin : Point
  origin.x := 0
  origin.y := 0
END

@r{Example 2: Record with Multiple Fields}
PROCEDURE PlayerRecord
BEGIN
  TYPE Player = RECORD
    name : STRING
    x : INTEGER
    y : INTEGER
    health : INTEGER
    score : DECIMAL
  END
  VAR hero : Player
  hero.name := "Hero"
  hero.health := 100
  hero.score := 0
END

@r{Example 3: Nested Records}
PROCEDURE NestedRecord
BEGIN
  TYPE Position = RECORD
    x : INTEGER
    y : INTEGER
  END
  TYPE Character = RECORD
    name : STRING
    pos : Position
    health : INTEGER
  END
  VAR player : Character
  player.pos.x := 160
  player.pos.y := 100
END

@r{Example 4: Array of Records}
PROCEDURE RecordArray
BEGIN
  TYPE Enemy = RECORD
    x : INTEGER
    y : INTEGER
    health : INTEGER
  END
  VAR enemies : ARRAY
  enemies[0].x := 100
  enemies[0].y := 100
END

@r{Example 5: Record Operations}
PROCEDURE RecordOps
BEGIN
  TYPE Sprite = RECORD
    x : INTEGER
    y : INTEGER
    width : INTEGER
    height : INTEGER
  END
  VAR sprite : Sprite
  sprite.x := sprite.x + 5
  sprite.y := sprite.y + 10
  sprite.width := 16
  sprite.height := 16
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Field Access:} Accessing non-existent fields causes errors.

@item @strong{Record Assignment:} Assigning one record to another copies all fields.

@item @strong{Nested Field Access:} Deeply nested field access uses chained dot notation.

@item @strong{Memory Layout:} Record field layout is backend-dependent; field access
patterns should not assume specific memory organization.
@end itemize

@node ARRAY Type
@section ARRAY Type (Indexed Collection)

@cindex ARRAY type
@cindex array index
@cindex subscript
@cindex multi-dimensional array

@subheading Purpose

ARRAY types provide indexed access to collections of elements of the same type.
Arrays support single and multi-dimensional indexing.

@subheading Syntax

@example
TYPE @var{name} = ARRAY
VAR @var{arr} : @var{type} ARRAY
@var{arr}[@var{index}] := @var{value}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Array}
PROCEDURE SimpleArray
BEGIN
  VAR scores : ARRAY
  scores[0] := 100
  scores[1] := 200
  scores[2] := 300
END

@r{Example 2: Array in Loop}
PROCEDURE InitArray
BEGIN
  VAR data : ARRAY
  VAR i : INTEGER
  FOR i FROM 0 TO 99 STEP 1 NEXT
    data[i] := i * 10
  END
END

@r{Example 3: Multi-Dimensional Array}
PROCEDURE Matrix
BEGIN
  VAR grid : ARRAY
  grid[0][0] := 1
  grid[0][1] := 2
  grid[1][0] := 3
  grid[1][1] := 4
END

@r{Example 4: DECIMAL Array}
PROCEDURE DecimalArray
BEGIN
  VAR prices : ARRAY
  prices[0] := 1000
  prices[1] := 2000
  prices[2] := 5000
END

@r{Example 5: Array Search}
PROCEDURE SearchArray
BEGIN
  VAR values : ARRAY
  VAR i : INTEGER
  VAR target : INTEGER
  target := 50
  FOR i FROM 0 TO 99 STEP 1 NEXT
    IF values[i] = target THEN
      PRINT "Found at " + i
      EXIT
    END
  END
END
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Bounds Checking:} Array access does not check bounds; out-of-bounds
access may corrupt memory or cause crashes.

@item @strong{Index Type:} Array indices must be integer expressions.

@item @strong{Memory Allocation:} Array memory allocation is backend-dependent;
arrays may be fixed-size or dynamically allocated.

@item @strong{Initialization:} Array elements have undefined initial values unless
explicitly initialized.

@item @strong{Sparse Arrays:} Accessing elements with a large gap between indices
may allocate memory for all intervening elements (depending on backend).
@end itemize

